# Untitled

A Tribo/Piezoelectric Nanogenerator Based on Bio-MOFs for Energy Harvesting and Antibacterial Wearable Device
New types of metal–organic framework (MOF) materials have great potential in solving the current global dilemma on energy, environment, and medical care. Herein, based on two kinds of biomolecule-MOFs (Bio-MOFs) with favorable biocompatibility and degradation-reconstruction characteristics, we have established a self-powered multi-functional device to achieve an efficient and broad-spectrum environmental energy collection and biomedical applications. Combining Zn(II) and carnosine-based Zn-Car_MOF possessing a high piezoelectric response \((d_{33}=11.17\,\mathsf{p m}\,\mathsf{V}^{-1})\) with patterned polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) film, a tribo-piezoelectric hybrid nanogenerator (TPHG) is constructed with a synergy output of triboelectric and piezoelectric effects. The Zn-Car_TPHG demonstrates a high output performance (131 V at \(100\,\mathsf{kPa}\)) and a wide range of pressure response \((1\,\mathsf{P a}{-}100\,\mathsf{kPa})\), possessing applications in environmental energy collection and biomedical sensors. To expand the application of the wearable device, a conductive hexagonal prism MOF \((\mathsf{C u}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,11\,\mathsf{h e x a h y d r o x y t r i p h e n y l e n e})_{2}\) (Cu-HHTP)) is synthesized and employed to load thymol (Thy). Cooperating with Zn-Car_TPHG, the resulting Cu-HHTP/Thy can achieve an efficient self-powered ROS (singlet oxygen \((^{1}0_{2})\) and hydroxyl radical (OH)) generation and drug synergistic broad-spectrum sterilization effect (efficiency \(\geq98\%\)). In a word, the flexible wearable device based on the multi-functional Bio-MOFs is sustainable and environmentally friendly, possessing wide application potential in fields of environmental energy collection, biosensors, and self-powered antibacterial.
## 1. Introduction
There is an imperious and increasing demand for novel types of lightweight, renewable, and continual energy supply devices. At present, mini and portable medical devices powered by
dry batteries are visible in diverse scenarios. However, the environmental pollution resulting from these energy suppliers, which are often based on heavy metal-based and rare-earth-based, poses significant concerns.[1,2] Nowadays, most environmental kinetic energy, such as wind energy,[3] tidal energy,[4] and rain droplet energy,[5] is converted into electric energy by turbine generators, with the problems of complexity, inconvenience, and insensitivity, rendering it unsuitable for portable applications.[6,7]
Therefore, it is significant to develop eco-friendly materials and new energy suppliers for the utilization of renewable and ubiquitous environmental kinetic energy to achieve a high-sensitivity and broad-spectrum energy collection capacity. Furthermore, exploring their potential applications in wearable medical devices is essential.
Among the new types of materials, metal-organic frameworks, which are self-assembled by the central metal and organic ligands, represent a unique class of crystalline organic–inorganic hybrid material with a periodic network structure.[8]
As porous materials, MOFs exhibit remarkable properties such as large specific surface area, high porosity, controllable structure, and biodegradability.[8,9] These characteristics endow them with significant potential for development in the fields of sensing,[10] catalysis,[11,12] drug delivery,[13] and other biomedical applications.[14–16] Most recently,
piezoelectric and conductive MOFs based on biomolecules and non-noble metal ions,[17,18] which have emerged as promising candidates for addressing environmental challenges due to their low toxicity, good biocompatibility, and environmental sustainability,[19,20] provide new prospects for environment energy collection and conversion as well as antibacterial applications.
As a widely existing form of energy in the environment, free kinetic energy represents a diverse and sustainable source of green energy with extensive potential uses.[21] The mechanical nanogenerator (MNG), particularly triboelectric nanogenerator
(TENG) and piezoelectric nanogenerators (PENG), have been developed as efficient devices capable of converting mechanical energy into electrical energy,[22–24] which possess the advantages of high performance, easy design and manufacture, and lightweight.[25,26] However, the application scope of a single triboelectric or piezoelectric device is constrained by its limited sensitivity and efficiency.[27] To address this challenge, scientists have proposed a hybrid strategy that leverages both triboelectric and piezoelectric effects based on TENG and PENG.[28] When subjected to mechanical stress, the tribo-piezoelectric hybrid nanogenerator combines surface charges generated through triboelectric response with polarized charges from piezoelectricity,[29] thereby achieving a higher charge density and enhanced output capacity. This innovation holds significant potential for efficient energy collection and self-powered sensing in various intelligent applications such as environmental energy collection systems and wearable monitoring devices).[30,31] In addition, to accommodate the demands of multi-application scenarios (including rain, tide, and sports), the TPHG with high sensitivity, wide detection range, good stability, favorable flexibility, and continuous durability is now being developed through various types of micropatterned designs.[32,33]
Herein, a flexible wearable device was designed based on two non-precious metals, renewable and environment-friendly BioMOFs, a piezoelectric Zn-Car MOF, and a conductive \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) - HHTP MOF. This innovative combination enables a broad-spectrum, highly sensitive environmental energy collection and self-powered antibacterial therapy. Cooperating the piezoelectric Zn-Car_MOF with the patterned PDMS film, a sandwichlike double-electrode flexible Zn-Car_TPHG is fabricated based on contact-separation mode. The Zn-Car_TPHG exhibits a high output performance, favorable stability, and wide-range sensitivity, positioning it as a novel lightweight and renewable energy supplier. In addition, to address the limitation of adjustable sterilization functionality in wearable devices, we have synthesized a hexagonal prism-shaped Cu-HHTP_MOF, whose special conductivity endows Cu-HHTP with \(2\mathrm{e^{-}}\) ORR electrocatalytic activity, which can generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and ROS \(\mathrm{~\textmu~}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and ·OH) under the electrifying condition. Therefore, with antibacterial molecule Thy depositing in the cavity of Cu-HHTP_MOF, CuHHTP/Thy can be loaded onto carbon fibers (CFs) and connected with a Zn-Car_TPHG through laser-induced graphene (LIG) circuit. This setup can effectively facilitate self-powered ROS oxidation damage and drug-releasing synergistic sterilization. When integrated into wearable devices, an efficient and broad-spectrum self-powered antibacterial therapy can be realized through the cooperation of Zn-Car_TPHG and Cu-HHTP Bio-MOFs. As a whole, based on two kinds of Bio-MOFs with fa
vorable biocompatibility, degradability and regeneration characteristics, and environmental friendliness, we have constructed a soft and portable device that serves as a demo to address the current global dilemma on energy, environment, and medical care (Scheme 1).
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Preparation and Characterization of Zn-Car_MOF
Zn-Car_MOF were synthesized via a solvothermal method utilizing \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) and carnosine according to the literature[34] (Figure 1a). As shown in Figures 1a,b and S1a–d (Supporting Information), \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) ions are tetrahedrally coordinated with a carboxyl group and an amino-terminal group on carnosine molecule as well as two nitrogen atoms of imidazole group to form a continual 4: 4 chemical structure. As indicated in scanning electron microscope (SEM) image, Zn-Car_MOF particles are regular spheres with an average diameter of \(4.2\pm0.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) , which are formed by the self-assembly of nanoscale plate crystals of MOFs with an average length of \(136.2\pm\:50.1\ \mathrm{nm}\) and an average width of \(24.0\,\pm\,5.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 1c). In addition, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of Zn-Car_MOF demonstrates multiple peaks at \(10.5\ ^{\circ}\) , \(12.0\ ^{\circ}\) , \(12.6\ ^{\circ}\) , \(19.8\ ^{\circ}\) , \(20.8\ ^{\circ}\) , \(21.1\ ^{\circ}\) , and \(21.9\ ^{\circ}\) , which align well with the simulation pattern of the single crystal structure of Zn-Car_MOF, indicating a successful synthesis of Zn-Car_MOF (Figure 1d). Besides, as indicated in Figures 1e and S2a–e (Supporting Information) obtained by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), two clear \(Z\!\mathrm{n}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) characteristic peaks at 1044.8 and \(1043.8~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(Z\!\mathrm{n}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) characteristic peak at \(1021.6\;\mathrm{eV}\) with a wide half-peak width can be observed, which correspond to the binding of \(Z\mathrm{n-N}\) at 1023.5 and \(1043.3\;\mathrm{eV}^{[35]}\) and the binding of \(Z\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{O}\) at 1021.2 and \(1044.8\;\mathrm{eV}^{[36]}\) indicating the formation of mixed \(Z\mathrm{n-N}\) and \(Z\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{O}\) coordination-bond in Zn-Car_MOF.
The result of dynamic light scattering (DLS) shows that the average size of Zn-Car particles is \(4.2\pm0.2\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) , which is similar to that observed via SEM (Figure S2b, Supporting Information inset). We further verified the chemical structure of Zn-Car_MOF by Fourier transform-infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and energy-dispersive X-ray spectrum (EDS) (Figure S2a,b, Supporting Information). In addition, the porous structure of Zn-Car particles was characterized by Brunauer–Emmett–Geller (BET), revealing that the specific surface area, average pore diameter, and total pore volume of Zn-Car particles are \(80.5\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , \(9.57\pm0.04\:\mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.253\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively (Figure 1f).
As demonstrated in Figure 1g, a piezoelectric response force microscope (PFM) was employed to investigate the piezoelectric property of Zn-Car particles. Upon applying various voltages to the tip of the PFM probe, the piezoelectric response of Zn-Car particles is obviously greater than that of the blank zone devoid of Zn-Car particles (Figures 1h–j and S3a–c, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the linear correlation between the applied voltage by the photodiode system and the planar piezoelectric response measured by PFM proves that Zn-Car exhibits apparent piezoelectric characteristics with a \(\mathrm{d}_{33}\) of \(11.17\ \mathrm{pm\ V^{-1}}\) (Figures 1k and S3d, Supporting Information). Compared with the effective piezoelectric coefficients of other inorganic piezoelectric materials, the \(\mathrm{d}_{33}\) coefficient of Zn-Car particles is similar
to that of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) (12.48 pm V\(^{-1}\)) and previously reported literature (12.95 pm V\(^{-1}\)).\[17,37\]
The degradation experiment conducted on Zn-Car particles shows that Zn-Car undergoes rapid degradation within 10 s under the action of concentrated nitric acid (Figure S4a,b, Supporting Information). After regeneration by solvothermal synthesis, the microstructure observed by SEM and the crystallization in the XRD pattern proves the complete regeneration of Zn-Car_MOF (Figure S4c–e, Supporting Information). Besides, compared with the original Zn-Car, the piezoelectric response and output performance of regenerated Zn-Car demonstrate minimal difference, revealing the stability of the piezoelectric characteristics of Zn-Car even after undergoing degradation and regeneration (Figure S4f, h, Supporting Information).
## 2.2. Construction and Performance of TPHG
Since the effect of triboelectrification is influenced by surface charges,\[38\] modifying the morphology of triboelectrification by microstructure patterning can enhance the effective contact area due to elastic characteristics under the condition of applied mechanical force,\[39,40\] thus promoting the surface charge density and further improving the output performance of triboelectrification. Consequently, we fabricated a surface patterned PDMS film with a micro-column array with a radius of 0.5 mm and a
height of 1 mm via a template-assisted method as demonstrated in Figures 2a,b and S5a,b, Supporting Information. The buckling instability of the slender micro-column maximizes the changes of contact area under external load,\[41\] thereby optimizing the output charging. Most importantly, as observed in Figure 2c, the patterned PDMS can be well twisted, stretched, and bent, portending a potential to be applied as a flexible and wearable device.
Utilizing the patterned PDMS dielectric layer and Zn-Car_MOF, a sandwich-like double-electrode TPHG structure was fabricated as shown in Figure 2d. In the proposed Zn-Car_TPHG, the triboelectric signal is generated through the contact-out mode interaction involving the flexible patterned PDMS film. Furthermore, by incorporating Zn-Car_MOF between a patterned PDMS layer and a smooth one, abundant frictional and piezoelectric charges can be induced by bending, deformation, and polarization of the working dipole. Due to the flexibility of PDMS, the assembled Zn-Car_TPHG can be twisted and bent. (Figure 2e). As illustrated in Figure 2f, when an external force is applied to the TPHG, it will polarize Zn-Car particles, resulting in most dipoles arranged along the direction of stress and generating an internal potential.\[42\] Combined with the triboelectric electrification generated by the friction of the flexible patterned PDMS film, it will enhance the overall charge density in the system. Then external electrons are attracted to the surface to balance the potential and generate mixed electrical
signals, thus converting mechanical energy into electrical energy.[43] Different from the traditional triboelectric nanogenerator,[44] the innovatively designed Zn-Car_TPHG achieves periodic piezoelectric and triboelectric mixed electrical signals obtained by nonlinear superposition of voltages generated by TENG and PENG in each deformation process, thereby enhancing the overall output electrical signals (Figure 2d,f).
An atomic-scale electron-cloud potential-well model was employed to describe the charge transfer between Zn-Car and PDMS (Figure 2f). According to Bader charge calculation results presented in Table S1 (Supporting Information), in the contact process between Zn-Car and PDMS, Zn-Car loses \(1.232\mathrm{~e~}\) and PDMS gains \(0.021\mathrm{~e~}\), indicating that the friction charge transfer occurs predominantly from Zn-Car to PDMS, suggesting PDMS possesses a higher electronegativity compared to Zn-Car. In addition, the charge differential density of Zn-Car@PDMS shows that during the interaction, Zn and Si atoms lose electrons, while O atoms gain electrons (Figures 2g,h and S6a–d, Supporting Information). Besides, the results of plane average charge density demonstrate distinct electron-losing peaks of Zn with the value of \(-0.06474\mathrm{~e~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) and electron-gaining peaks of O with the value of \(0.07879\mathrm{~e~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) on the interface between Zn-Car and PDMS (Figure 2i). Since Zn-Car material does not exhibit 2D piezoelectric properties or semiconductor characteristics, its band gap properties are not reflected in the density of states (DOS) diagram (Figure S6e,f, Supporting Information).
Supporting Information). Besides, the results of plane average charge density demonstrate distinct electron-losing peaks of Zn with the value of \(-0.06474\mathrm{~e~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) and electron-gaining peaks of O with the value of \(0.07879\mathrm{~e~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) on the interface between Zn-Car and PDMS (Figure 2i). Since Zn-Car material does not exhibit 2D piezoelectric properties or semiconductor characteristics, its band gap properties are not reflected in the density of states (DOS) diagram (Figure S6e,f, Supporting Information).
A typical contact-separation mode is adopted to verify the performance of the TPHG.[45,46] As indicated in Figure 2j,k, the open circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{OC}})\) and short circuit current \((I_{\mathrm{SC}})\) of the \(2\times2\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG with patterned PDMS is 1.50 and 1.45 times higher than that of Zn-Car_TPHG without pattern under the identical pressure conditions, respectively. With the increase of external loading resistance from \(10\,\mathrm{k}\Omega\) to \(10\,\mathrm{G}\Omega\), the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of a \(2\times2\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG increases from 0.1 to \(40\,\mathrm{V}\), while the output current decreases from 1.77 to \(0.08\,\upmu\mathrm{A}\) (Figure 2l),
and the output power density corresponding to different loading resistance reveals a maximum power density of \(6.32~\upmu\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 2m), which meets the requirements of miniature electronic products such as pacemakers, blood pressure monitoring sensors and gait sensors.[47–49]
As shown in Figure S7e (Supporting Information), with the increase of the area of Zn-Car_TPHG (from \(0.5 \times 0.5\) to \(3 \times 3~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)), the generated \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) increases from 14.6 to \(68.7~\mathrm{V}\). In addition, an automatic circulating pressure device was utilized to apply a \(2 \times 2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG with a constant frequency \((0.5{-}2~\mathrm{Hz})\) and preset force \((1{-}100~\mathrm{kPa})\). As presented in Figure S7g, Supporting Information, and Figure 2n, with the increase in tapping frequency, the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) remains generally stable. This stability arises because the voltage solely depends on the separation and friction charge density of the two friction materials, and it does not involve any dynamic friction charge transfer processes under open circuit conditions.[50] Besides, under the consistent tapping frequency and force, Zn-Car_TPHG maintains a stable \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) within an extensive testing range, indicating its wide operating window. In addition, the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) was positively correlated with the tapping force, reaching \(131~\mathrm{V}\) at \(100~\mathrm{kPa}\), indicating that the effective contact area between the patterned PDMS and the substrate gradually increased with the contact force.
As shown in Figures 2o and S8a–e (Supporting Information), the resulting Zn-Car_TPHG device maintains a stable \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) throughout 9000 contact-separation cycles and different tensile strains \((10\%,\ 30\%)\) with different frequencies \((0.5~\mathrm{Hz},\ 1~\mathrm{Hz})\). This stability confirms the structural integrity and mechanical stability of Zn-Car_TPHG for sustained use. In addition, the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of Zn-Car_TPHG remains stable after enduring 50 times repeated wetting-drying and long-term soaking in water (Figure 2p), further confirming the waterproofing and durability of Zn-Car_TPHG. At the same time, the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(2 \times 2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG maintains stability at both ambient temperature \((25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and high temperatures \((60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), while still delivering impressive output performance \((14.6~\mathrm{V})\) at the extreme temperatures of \(-20\) and \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 2q).
Therefore, it is proved that Zn-Car_TPHG has a superior and reliable tribo-piezoelectric hybrid output performance in various situations, possessing a broad working range, appropriate extensibility, and durability. These attributes suggest that the prepared Zn-Car_TPHG holds promising potential in practical applications and can serve as an efficient, long-lasting wearable device.
## 2.3. Energy Collection from Environments and Gait Detection by Zn-Car_TPHG
Zn-Car_TPHG boasts a straightforward structure and effortless installation on any outdoor surface, making it ideal for rain droplet energy harvesting (Figure 3a). The minimum detection limit of Zn-Car_TPHG is \(1~\mathrm{Pa}\) with an \(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(3.0 \times 10^{-3}~\upmu\mathrm{A}\) (Figure 3b), demonstrating a remarkable perception of micropressure \((0.4~\mathrm{mN})\). As shown in Figures 3c and S10a (Supporting Information), water droplets \((60~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) water droplets, dripping from the height of \(20~\mathrm{cm}\), at a frequency of \(1.5~\mathrm{Hz}\)) can trigger a \(2 \times 2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG to generate a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.18~\mathrm{V}\) and an \(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(1.5 \times 10^{-2}~\upmu\mathrm{A}\). Furthermore, a spray device \((10~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) spraying water, spraying from a height of \(20~\mathrm{cm}\)) was applied to simulate
the rain environment, achieving a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(3.90~\mathrm{V}\) and an \(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(0.11~\upmu\mathrm{A}\) with an output power density of \(0.11~\upmu\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Figures 3c and S10b, Supporting Information). The rain energy collected by TPHG is sufficient to charge a \(1~\upmu\mathrm{F}\) capacitor, with the voltage of the capacitor increasing by \(0.04~\mathrm{V}\) after \(100~\mathrm{s}\), demonstrating a positive correlation relationship between the capacitor voltage and charging time (Figure 3d). After \(12~\mathrm{h}\) of charging with Zn-Car_TPHG, six LED bulbs were successfully illuminated (Figure S10c,d).
In view of the sensitivity of Zn-Car_TPHG to tiny pressure and extensive operational range, Zn-Car_TPHG is employed as a piezo-triboelectric device to collect environmental energy in the ocean and electric-bicycle driving to power various electronic devices (Figure 3e). Under the conditions of simulating floating on the ocean surface or washed ashore by tides on the beach (Figure 3f), Zn-Car_TPHG can obtain a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of 0.55 and \(0.51~\mathrm{V}\), along with an \(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of 0.015 and \(0.012~\upmu\mathrm{A}\), respectively (Figures 3g,h and S10e,f, Supporting Information). In addition, the capacitors can be charged by Zn-Car_TPHG under wave conditions sufficiently to illuminate six LED bulbs after \(12~\mathrm{h}\) charging (Figure S10g,h, Supporting Information). Besides, Zn-Car_TPHG (with an area of \(10 \times 10~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) was used to collect the energy from the electric bicycle (Figure 3i). At a speed of \(5~\mathrm{km}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) reaches \(51.4~\mathrm{V}\), which escalates to \(124.6~\mathrm{V}\) at \(25~\mathrm{km}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (Figures 3j and S10i,j, Supporting Information). At the same time, Zn-Car_TPHG consistently charge capacitors (1, 4.7, 10, 47, 100, and \(220~\upmu\mathrm{F}\)) stably under regular compression \((P=10~\mathrm{kPa}, f=2~\mathrm{Hz})\), exhibiting a linear voltage increase with charging time (Figure 3k). As shown in Figure 3l, the capacitor can be repeatedly charged to \(3~\mathrm{V}\) and discharged to light an LED bulb. After a mere \(30~\mathrm{min}\) of charging, \(20~\mathrm{LED}\) bulbs can be lit up by the capacitor, which can also serve as a power source for clocks, calculators, and small fans. In general, Zn-Car_TPHG can effectively and stably collect energy from various environmental energy sources with a wide range to provide energy for mini devices.
Equipped with Bluetooth, the Zn-Car_TPHG can be securely attached to the insole and utilized as a gait monitor to track various human body postures (Figures 3m and S11a, Supporting Information). A \(2 \times 2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG is attached to the heel area to detect multiple signals of a series of actions such as walking, running, stepping, tiptoeing, and jumping (Figures 3n and S11b, c, Supporting Information). Generally, a gait cycle includes four processes named heel contact, toe contact, heel departure, and toe departure.[51] During forward walking, two positive peaks representing heel contact and toe contact are observed, followed by two negative peaks representing heel and toe separation (Figures 3n and S11d, Supporting Information). In addition, several refined features, such as the shape, amplitude, and time interval of adjacent cycles, can be extracted from the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of Zn-Car_TPHG to distinguish detailed sports physiological information, indicating its advanced ability in human motion posture monitoring.
As shown in Figure 3o, according to the anatomical structure of the foot and the contact area with the ground,[52] we selected four positions in the forefoot, midfoot of the heel, and metatarsal region, and utilized four \(2 \times 2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHGs to test the outputs of these different areas (Figure S11e, f, Supporting Information). The magnitude and waveform of voltage and current
signals generated at different locations are significantly different (Figures 3o,p and S11g, Supporting Information), indicating that Zn-Car_TPHG can be employed to monitor different gait and movement conditions effectively (Figure S11h, Supporting Information). In addition, as shown in Figure S11i–l (Supporting Information), the repeated friction experiment conducted on Zn-Car-TPHG embedded in the insole revealed a stable output performance under repeated friction and long-term use, confirming its durability when integrated into insoles. Additionally, Zn
Car-TPHG can maintain a stable voltage signal when exposed to sweat, and dust environments, highlighting its great anti-sweat and anti-pollution ability, demonstrating the potential of Zn-CarTPHG for energy harvesting and biosensor applications. Consequently, multiple Zn-Car_TPHGs connected to the different positions of soles can be applied to analyze the detailed sports information and signals of various gait activities. This approach holds potential for physiological monitoring and disease prevention, providing a promising scheme for medical diagnosis combined
with artificial intelligence and remote monitoring based on the Internet.
## 2.4. Structural Characterization and ROS Producing Performance of Cu-HHTP MOF
To achieve enhanced energy transmission and biomedical application, the conductive Cu-HHTP MOF was synthesized based on copper acetate and HHTP as the previously reported approach,[53] obtaining a graphene-like honeycomb porous layered MOF (Figures 4a,b and S12a–f, Supporting Information). SEM and transmission electron microscope (TEM) images indicate that the synthesized Cu-HHTP MOF possesses a regular hexagonal prism shape (Figures 4c and S13a–c, Supporting Information), with an average diameter of \(212.2\pm30.5\ \mathrm{nm}\) and a length of \(1.4\pm0.4\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) . The corresponding element distribution mapping indicates that C, O, and Cu elements are uniformly distributed in Cu-HHTP MOF (Figure 4d).
As shown in the XRD pattern (Figure 4e), Cu-HHTP powders present obvious diffraction peaks at \(4.7\,^{\circ}\) , \(9.5\,^{\circ}\) , \(12.6\,^{\circ}\) , and \(16.4\,^{\circ}\) , corresponding to the (100), (200), (210), and (220) planes of 2D \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}\) crystal, respectively, and the strong diffraction peak at \(27.9\,^{\circ}\) correspond to the (001) plane. In addition, XPS spectra of Cu-HHTP MOF show the \(\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak and its satellite peak at 931.8 and \(933.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) , and \(\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) peak and its satellite peak at 951.5 and \(953.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) , respectively (Figure 4f), indicating that Cu element in Cu-HHTP MOF exists in a mixed valence state of \(+1\) and \(+2\) . The chemical bonds and chemical states of Cu-HHTP MOF were further studied by FTIR, XPS, and UV–vis spectrum, which confirmed the 3:2 coordination between \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and HHTP ligand (Figure S14a–e, Supporting Information).
The isotherm of Cu-HHTP MOF measured by BET conforms to the type I adsorption behavior, indicating a specific surface area of \(538.4\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and an average pore size of \(1.23\pm0.02\ \mathrm{nm}\) , respectively (Figure 4g), confirming that it is a microporous material with a large specific surface area. In addition, 2D conjugated Cu-HHTP MOF is composed of a metal center and conjugated ligand, which can facilitate electron jumping or band transport,[54] thereby imparting its unique conductivity. As shown in Figure 4h, the average conductivity of pure Cu-HHTP powders tested by the four-probe method was \(1.63\pm0.31\ \mathrm{mS}\ \mathrm{m}^{-1}\) . With the increased mass fraction of Cu-HHTP in agarose \((0.03\%{-}0.24\%)\) , the conductivity of the hydrogel increased from 0.2 to \(0.75\ \mathrm{mS\ m^{-1}}\) .
The carbon paper loaded with Cu-HHTP MOF on the cathode was subjected to electrification to assess its capacity to generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and ROS (Figure 4i). The oxygen reduction peak at \(0.18\ \mathrm{V}\) by Cu-HHTP (Figure S15c, Supporting Information) confirms that \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is reduced to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) at the active site of Cu-HHTP electrode. 2, 2, 6, 6-tetramethyl-1-piperidine (TEMP) and 5, 5-dimethyl-1-pyrrolidine N-oxide (DMPO) were employed as \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH capture agent, respectively. Figure 4j,k clearly exhibits characteristic peaks for \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH upon electrifying Cu-HHTP MOF. Notably, the signals indicative of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH generation are significantly intensified compared to their nonelectrified counterparts, suggesting that Cu-HHTP MOF can generate substantial quantities of both species under electrified conditions. This can be attributed to the electrocatalytic perfor
mance of Cu-HHTP MOF,[55] which produces active \(\mathrm{e}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{h^{+}}\) (Figure S16a, Supporting Information). The generated \(\mathrm{h^{+}}\) oxidizes water molecules into OH, while the \(\mathrm{e}^{-}\) converts \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into intermediate superoxide anion radical \((\mathrm{O}^{2-})\) , which is further oxidized by \(\mathrm{h^{+}}\) to generate \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) . The catalase activity of Cu-HHTP MOF can activate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in situ at the \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) active site on the cathode to produce additional ROS (Figure \(\mathrm{S16a}_{1}{-}\mathrm{a}_{2}\) , Supporting Information).
Employing diphenylisobenzofuran (DPBF) as a \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) scavenger, the characteristic absorption peak of DPBF at \(413\ \mathrm{nm}\) undergoes a significant decline with the increase of the unit load \((0{-}2.0\ \mathrm{mg\ cm}^{-2})\) of Cu-HHTP MOF, confirming the ability of Cu-HHTP MOF to produce \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) under electrical conditions (Figures 4l–n and S16e–g, Supporting Information). In addition, the characteristic absorption peak of the absorption spectrum also presents an obvious downward trend with the increase of electrifying time \((0{-}24\ \mathrm{h})\) and electrifying voltage \((0{-}6\ \mathrm{V})\) . Similarly, methylene blue (MB) served as an indicator to verify the generation of OH (Figures 4a and S16a, Supporting Information). With the increase of unit load, electrifying time, and electrifying voltage of Cu-HHTP MOF, the characteristic absorption peak at \(665\ \mathrm{nm}\) of MB diminishes gradually (Figures 4o–q and S16h–j, Supporting Information), indicating that Cu-HHTP MOF efficiently generates OH to degrade MB when electrically stimulated. In addition, the generation of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH is further verified by pulse electrification powered by Zn-Car_1TPHG (Figures 4r,s and S16k,l, Supporting Information). The absorption peaks of DPBF and MB decrease significantly under the electrified stages, while the changes in absorption peaks of DPBF and MB in the nonelectrified stages are substantially less pronounced than those in the electrified stages, proving that Cu-HHTP, as a MOF-based electrocatalyst, has high activity of generating \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH.
Cu-HHTP can be rapidly degraded in the solution of \(1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}\ \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}\) within \(1\ \mathrm{min}\) (Figure S18a,b, Supporting Information). After regeneration by solvothermal synthesis, the microstructure observed by SEM and the crystallization in the XRD pattern, as well as the electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH comparisons of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH between the regenerated and original Cu-HHTP, prove the complete regeneration of Cu-HHTP (Figure S18c–g, Supporting Information).
## 2.5. Synthesis, Characterization, and Antibacterial Properties of Cu-HHTP/Thy
Cu-HHTP resulting from the coordination of \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and HHTP has a hexagonal topological space structure with a macrocyclic cavity diameter of \(1.23\ \mathrm{nm}\) , which is capable of accommodating various small molecule drugs.[56] As shown in Figure 5a, Thy, an excellent antibiosis monoterpene phenol,[57] is incorporated into the porous structure of Cu-HHTP by a straight physical adsorption method.[58] The results of SEM and TEM demonstrate numerous aggregated and layered Thy particles adorning the surface of rod-shaped Cu-HHTP (Figures 5b and S19a, Supporting Information). Upon loading Thy, Cu-HHTP/Thy displays a notably enhanced absorbance at \(277\ \mathrm{nm}\) in the UV–vis spectrum compared to unladen Cu-HHTP, indicating the successful adsorption of Thy (Figure S19b, Supporting Information). In addition, the BET analysis (Figure 5c) shows that the surface
area of Cu-HHTP decreases from 538.4 to \(108.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) postloading, accompanied by a reduction in total pore volume from 0.730 to \(0.018\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , indicating that the large channel of CuHHTP_MOF serves as an effective loading carrier for Thy. In addition, there is an endothermic peak at \(55\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (corresponding to the melting peak of Thy) in the differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) thermogram of Cu-HHTP/Thy (Figure 5d).
According to the standard UV-vis spectrum curve of Thy (Figure S19e,f, Supporting Information), the amount of Thy loaded on Cu-HHTP is \(78~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\) \((7.8\%\) loading capacity). As shown in Figure 5f, Cu-HHTP/Thy samples present a similar trend of initial rapid release in the first \(3\,\mathrm{{h}}\) under various conditions \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\) , \(\mathrm{pH}=5.5\) , and co-incubation with Candida albicans (C. albicans), followed by a sustained release in the next \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) . Thy in Cu-HHTP/Thy under the condition of \(\mathrm{pH}=5.5\) shows a faster initial release \((70.2\%\) at \(3\mathrm{~h~}\) ) and higher cumulative release \((79.6\%\) at \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) ) compared to neutral \(\mathrm{pH}\) (7.4), which shows a \(58.4\%\) initial release at \(3\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and \(69.1\%\) cumulative release at \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) . This \(\mathrm{pH}\) responsiveness may be attributed to the partial decomposition of Cu-HHTP structure under acidic conditions and the
release of Thy from the cavities of Cu-HHTP. In addition, when co-incubated with C. albicans, Thy in Cu-HHTP/Thy maintains a prolonged and more effective release profile, achieving a cumulative release of \(98.8\%\) at \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) , which could be due to the continuous consumption of Thy by C. albicans.
To verify the effective antibiosis properties of Cu-HHTP/Thy against pathogens, we conducted experiments using the solid medium with various electrifying times (0–1200 s) and various contents of Cu-HHTP/Thy (50, 100, 150, 200, \(300~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) (Figures 5g,h and S21a–d, Supporting Information). The colony forming unit (CFU) on the culture plate was observed to calculate the viable bacteria cultured on the Cu-HHTP/Thy medium. As shown in Figure 5g, most bacteria live well on the original medium without electricity. When these media with an addition of \(200~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Cu-HHTP/Thy were electrified by a \(2~\times\) \(2~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) Zn-Car_TPHG, the number of viable E. coli decreased significantly with the increase of electrifying time, reaching a reduction rate of \(98.2\%\) at \(600~\mathrm{s}\) (Figure 5h). In addition, with the increase of Cu-HHTP/Thy addition \((50{-}300~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) , the antibacterial ratio with an electrifying time of \(600~\mathrm{s}\) improves
from \(26.1\%\) to \(99.2\%\). (Figure S21b, Supporting Information). Similarly, S. aureus treated with \(200~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Cu-HHTP/Thy medium (electrified for \(600\;\mathrm{s}\)) obtains a reduction ratio of \(98.0\%\) (Figures 5h and S21d, Supporting Information).
As shown in Figures 5g,h and S21e,f (Supporting Information), Cu-HHTP/Thy demonstrated similar results for C. albicans as it did against E. coli and S. aureus. Specifically, the colony number decreases with the increase of electrifying time and the amount of Cu-HHTP/Thy. Specifically, a higher antibacterial effect \((98.6\%)\) can be obtained when the culture medium supplemented with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Cu-HHTP/Thy was electrified for \(600\;\mathrm{s}\), indicating the remarkable antifungal activity of Cu-HHTP/Thy system, attributed to the superior antifungal activity of Thy.[57,59] Therefore, Cu-HHTP/Thy exhibits broad-spectrum antibiosis abilities under electrical conditions, making it a promising candidate for the treatment of bacterial and fungal infections.
For a direct visualization, bacterial and fungal culture solutions were treated with \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Cu-HHTP/Thy with or without electrifying, followed by co-staining with Syto 9/propidium iodide (PI). As demonstrated in Figure 5i,j, the blank or nonelectrified Cu-HHTP/Thy culture medium exhibited a significant number of living bacteria stained with bright green fluorescence. In contrast, the electrified Cu-HHTP/Thy culture medium showed only a few weak greenes with a bacterial viability of merely \(14.5\%\) , \(4.3\%\) , and \(1.6\%\) (E. coli, S. aureus, and C. albicans) respectively, and obtains a large number of red fluorescence, indicating that ROS produced by Cu-HHTP/Thy lead to the rupture of cell membrane and infiltration of PI.[60] This observation is consistent with the generally accepted antibacterial mechanism of dynamic therapy based on ROS,[61,62] proving that \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH produced by Cu-HHTP/Thy can effectively induce oxidative damage to microbial membranes, thereby exerting an antimicrobial effect.
The biocompatibility of Cu-HHTP/Thy was evaluated by cellcounting-kit-8 (CCK-8) assay. The results revealed minimal cytotoxicity with cell viability exceeding \(95.8\%\) when the concentration of Cu-HHTP/Thy was below \(100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (Figure S24a–c, Supporting Information). After the concentration of CuHHTP/Thy increased to over \(200~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , the cell viability decreased significantly, reaching merely \(24.2\%\) of the original value on day 3 at the concentration of \(800~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , indicating cytotoxicity at high concentrations \((\geq200~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) . In addition, the hemolysis assay of Cu-HHTP/Thy demonstrates favorable red blood cell compatibility co-cultured with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) CuHHTP/Thy. (Figure S24d, Supporting Information). As demonstrated in Figure S24e, f (Supporting Information), the staining results of live-dead cells indicate that the cells in all the groups are in good condition with green coloration and spindle shapes, demonstrating an excellent biocompatibility of Cu-HHTP/Thy at a low concentration for biomedical prospects.
## 2.6. Preparation of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs and Assembly of Wearable Antimicrobial Device
A conductive composite fabric stitched with Cu-HHTP/Thy and carbon fibers (CFs) was prepared according to the approach presented in Figure 6a. As shown in Figure 6b, the surface of the
original CFs is smooth and flat. Through an in-situ hydrothermal process,[53] Cu-HHTP grows onto the CFs, resulting in an irregular distribution of hexagonal prismatic rod-like structures on the surface. These Cu-HHTP-coated CFs were then immersed in a Thy ethanol solution to incorporate Thy within the CuHHTP_MOF structure.
As shown in Figure 6c–e, due to the low conductivity of CuHHTP on the surface of CFs, the conductivity of Cu-HHTP/CFs is reduced to \(0.26\;\mathrm{~S~}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\) , which is significantly lower than that of pure CFs at \(0.71\;\mathrm{~S~m}^{-1}\) . Meanwhile, upon loading nonconductive Thy on the of Cu-HHTP/CFs, the conductivity of CuHHTP/Thy CFs showed no significant difference \((0.21\;\mathrm{~S~m}^{-1})\) to Cu-HHTP/CFs, and Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs still maintains sufficient conductivity to illuminate an LED bulb when spun onto cotton cloth (Figure 6e). Therefore, Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs can be seamlessly integrated into clothes (such as sock toes) to obtain composite clothes with conductivity and antibacterial properties (Figure 6a,f).
In addition, by assembling the energy-supplying ZnCar_TPHG and Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs conductive socks, a selfpowered wearable device for medical antibacterial therapy has been developed (Figure 6g,h). Zn-Car_TPHG was installed at the heel, while a circuit carved by laser-induced graphene (LIG) was integrated with Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs conductive socks for energy transmission. This setup facilitates the electrocatalytic ROS production and Thy releasing synergistic antibacterial assay (Figure 6i). As shown in Figure 6j,k, with the increase of loading area of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs and the extension of electrifying time, the sterilization effect of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs fabric is gradually improved. Specifically, when the loading area reaches \(2\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) , the antibacterial ratios against E. coli and S. aureus reach \(98.6\%\) and \(98.2\%\) respectively after being electrified for \(600\;\mathrm{s}\) by Zn-Car_TPHG, indicating the significant antibacterial property of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs. In addition, the antibacterial efficiency on C. albicans can reach \(98.0\%\) after \(600\;\mathrm{s}\) of electrifying when the loading area of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs is \(1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) , owing to the excellent antifungal properties of Thy.[59]
Then, the pigskin was utilized to assess the antibacterial performance of the integrated TPHG-LIG power supply circuit and Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs conductive fabric (Figure 6l). As presented in Figure 6m, upon loading \(2\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs, its antibacterial effects on E. coli, S. aureus, and C. albicans reach \(95.4\%\) , \(98.5\%\) , and \(98.1\%\) respectively, indicating a broad-spectrum antibacterial ability against various microorganisms. In addition, effective measures such as prolonging the charging time, increasing the electrode area, and loading of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs can be taken to further promote sterilization efficiency.
However, there are still some shortcomings in this study, such as the lack of exploring an innovative MOF to produce an ultrahigh output performance of the device compared with the reported TENGs.[22,23] Moreover, greater attention should be given to refining the microstructure, ultrastructural characteristics, and molecular mechanisms that significantly impact PENG/TENG performance. Thirdly, as a clear application-oriented technology and equipment, careful consideration and optimization are necessary to achieve better low-cost mass production and commercialization. Therefore, it is significant to strive to improve the output performance of the constructed wearable devices and explore new types of MOF-based
triboelectric/piezoelectric nanogenerators, as well as explore their multifunctional applications in the fields of energy collection and wearable devices in more depth.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we have constructed a flexible wearable device based on two kinds of non-noble, renewable, and environment-friendly Bio-MOFs: the piezoelectric Zn-Car_MOF and the conductive Cu-HHTP_MOF. This innovation serves as a demo to address the current global dilemma on energy, environment, and medical care. By integrating Zn-Car particles between a patterned PDMS layer and a smooth one, a sandwich-like double-electrode flexible TPHG structure is fabricated that combines both triboelectric and piezoelectric effects for enhanced output. The Zn-Car_TPHG boasts impressive high output performance (131 V at \(100\;\mathrm{kPa}\)), stability in various environments, and a wide range of pressure response \((1\ \mathrm{Pa}{-}100\ \mathrm{kPa})\), making it suitable for broad-spectrum energy collection and human motion monitoring, providing a lightweight, renewable and green energy supplying wearable device solution. In addition, the synthesized hexagonal prism-shaped Cu-HHTP_MOF characterized by its unique conductivity and \(2\mathrm{e^{-}}\) ORB electrocatalytic activity is able to generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and ROS \(({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and OH) under the electrifying condition. With Thy loaded in the cavity of Cu-HHTP_MOF, it is combined with the self-powered flexible Zn-Car_TPHG device, realizing an efficient ROS oxidative damage and drug-releasing synergistic broad-spectrum sterilization therapy. The flexible wearable device has the potential to combine with deep learning and smart home applications, facilitating further energy collection in diverse natural settings and providing comprehensive user information through gait monitoring and recognition, which demonstrates great prospects in medical diagnosis combined with artificial intelligence and remote monitoring based on the Internet.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis of Zn-Car-MOF: Zn-Car_MOF was prepared by the solvothermal method. Initially, carnosine powder and \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) \(6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) crystal were dissolved in water to achieve concentrations of 80 and \(160~\mathsf{m m}\) respectively. Carnosine solution \((2\,\mathsf{\ m L},\ 80\,\mathsf{\ m M})\) , \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) solution (1 mL, \(160\;\mathsf{m M})\) , solvent ( \(10~\mathsf{m L}\) DMF), and water \((2\ m\mathsf{L})\) were mixed in a \(20~\mathsf{m L}\) scintillation vial. The mixture was then subjected to intense ultrasound for thorough mixing. Then the vial was put in an oven and heated to \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rising rate of \(1\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{\,-1}\) and kept for \(12\,\mathfrak{h}\) , then cooled to \(25~^{\circ}\subset\) at \(1\ {}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{\,-1}\) . Finally, the obtained crystals were washed several times with ethanol and deionized water, and then the mixture was centrifuged at 6000 rpm for \(10\ \mathsf{m i n}\) and the supernatant was removed. The precipitate was dried at \(60~^{\circ}\subset\) for \(12~\mathsf{h}\) . The obtained products were collected in sample bottles, yielding \(65\%\) of the expected product.
Manufacturing of Patterned PDMS and Zn-Car_TPHG: We use the epoxy resin mold with a concave structure on the surface constructed via a template-assisted method to mold PDMS. The prepolymer A (mainly composed of poly (Dimethyl methyl siloxane) and cross-linking agent B (prepolymer with vinyl side chain) in a ratio of 10:1 was evenly mixed and poured on the surface of the mold. It was then cured in an oven at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(6\;\mathrm{h}\) . Upon demolding, a PDMS film with a micro-column array with a radius of \(0.5\ \mathrm{mm}\) and a height of \(1\ \mathsf{m m}\) was obtained, and the distance between each micro-column was \(1\ \mathsf{m m}\) . In addition, the Zn-Car_TPHG tribo-piezoelectric hybrid nanogenerator was prepared based on patterned PDMS and Zn-Car piezoelectric particles. Specifically, a \(3\ \mathsf{m m}\) thick pat
terned PDMS with micro-columns was employed as the top triboelectric layer, while a \(1\ \mathsf{m m}\) thick double-sided adhesive tape acted as the spacer and a \(3\ \mathsf{m m}\) thick PDMS film as the bottom dielectric layer, and \(20~\mathsf{m g}\) Zn-Car particles were loaded between the two PDMS layers as the piezoelectric layer. Aluminum foil and copper tape were added to the back of two layers of PDMS as electrodes, and the outermost layer was encapsulated with polyimide tape.
Synthesis of Cu-HHTP and Cu-HHTP/Thy: HHTP (1.5 mL, \(0.22\;\mathsf{m M}\) ), \(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{2}\) \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) (65.8 mg, \(0.33\;\mathsf{m M}\) ), DMF (1.5 mL), and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) (15 mL) were thoroughly mixed in a \(20~\mathsf{m L}\) scintillation vial. After ultrasonic treatment for \(15\;\mathsf{m i n}\) , the vial was heated at \(85\,^{\circ}\subset\) for \(12\,\mathsf{h}\) to obtain a black suspension. Upon cooling to \(25~^{\circ}\subset\) , black powder was collected by filtration, washed with deionized water and methanol, and then dried in an oven at \(60~^{\circ}\subset\) to obtain black Cu-HHTP crystals. In addition, Cu-HHTP/Thy was prepared by the physical adsorption method reported previously. The synthesized \(1\ \mathsf{m g\ C u-H H T P}\) crystal was dispersed in \(1\ \mathsf{m L}\) ethanol solution containing \(20\,\mathsf{m g}\,\mathsf{T h y}\) , which was shaken overnight to ensure complete dispersion of Thy within the cavities and on the surface of Cu-HHTP. Then the mixture was centrifuged at \(8000\;\mathsf{r p m}\) for \(10\ \mathsf{m i n}\) and the supernatant was removed. The precipitate was dried at \(25~^{\circ}\subset\) to obtain black Cu-HHTP/Thy crystal with grass fragrance.
The Thy loading capacity and efficiency were determined by UV–Vis spectrophotometer at \(277\ \mathsf{n m}\) wavelength. The calculation formula for loading capacity is as follows:
\[
\mathsf{L o a d i n g~c a p a c i t y}=\frac{M_1}{M_0}\times100\%
\]
where \(M_{0}\) is the weight of Cu-HHTP and \(M_{1}\) is the weight of Thy in CuHHTP/Thy.
Preparation of Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs and Composite Fabrics: First, Commercial CFs (consisting of \(1\ \mathsf{k}\) strands) loaded with Cu-HHTP were prepared by hydrothermal reaction and one-pot stewing. The CFs were immersed in a precursor solution containing \(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})\) and HHTP, allowing the growth of Cu-HHTP hexagonal crystals onto their surfaces via hydrothermal synthesis. Then, Cu-HHTP CFs were soaked in the ethanol solution containing Thy, and the CFs loaded with Cu-HHTP/Thy were obtained upon drying. Finally, the Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs composite fabric with electrical conductivity was obtained by sewing Cu-HHTP/Thy CFs onto cotton fabric.
Consent for Participate and Publication: All participants were provided with information regarding the study and gave their written informed consent prior to participation. Informed consent was waived by the Institutional Review Board because the study was not for human research, so the assigned study/project number was not applicable.
